# @@EXTENSION_ID@@

This is a standalone Exaudio extension repository owned by `@@OWNER@@/@@REPOSITORY@@`.
It contains real starter functionality: a panel and command that insert a greeting in one
undoable document transaction, plus empty-segment diagnostics. Customize that implementation
and the manifest before publishing your extension.

The SDK dependency is fetched from `@@SDK_URL@@`, pinned to full commit
`@@SDK_COMMIT@@`. There are **no relative dependencies on a Exaudio checkout**. Cargo checks
out the complete SDK repository, so the SDK's internal WIT files remain available. The same
commit supplies the release validator and packaging CLI in CI. The generator does not
claim that a supplied repository/commit is already public: the maintainer must supply a
real accessible published commit containing `exaudio-sdk` and `exaudio-extension-index`.

## Build and test locally

Install the pinned Rust toolchain from `rust-toolchain.toml`, then run:

```text
rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown
cargo install --locked --version 1.239.0 wasm-tools
cargo install --locked --git @@SDK_URL@@ --rev @@SDK_COMMIT@@ exaudio-extension-index
cargo build --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown
wasm-tools component new target/wasm32-unknown-unknown/release/@@CRATE_ARTIFACT@@.wasm -o module.wasm
wasm-tools validate module.wasm
exaudio-extension-index pack manifest.json module.wasm @@EXTENSION_ID@@-@@VERSION@@.exaudio LICENSE
exaudio-extension-index preview-developer @@EXTENSION_ID@@-@@VERSION@@.exaudio
```

Commit the generated `Cargo.lock` before tagging a release so dependency resolution is
reviewable and reproducible. If the first CI build has no lockfile, it generates one and
attaches the exact resolved lockfile to the release as `build-Cargo.lock`; this records the
actual build inputs but does not pretend those transitive dependencies were pinned in the
source commit. Subsequent releases should use a committed lockfile.

Load the archive as a developer source in Exaudio, explicitly approve its SHA256 and requested
permissions, and exercise its panel/command/diagnostics. The example requests document read
and edit only. Declaring permissions does not grant them. Runtime/ABI validation and your
functional tests remain necessary; archive validation alone is not an extension test.

## Versioned GitHub releases

The included `.github/workflows/release.yml` runs only when you push a `v*` tag to this
repository. It checks the repository identity and exact `v<manifest version>` tag, builds
the real WASM component, validates and packages it, and generates the index record from the
actual archive bytes and source commit. The publish job creates a new GitHub release with:

- `@@EXTENSION_ID@@-VERSION.exaudio`: the installable package;
- `index-entry.json`: the actual catalog extension/release record;
- `build-Cargo.lock`: exact resolved Rust dependencies;
- `build-source.json`: author source and SDK commit identities and component-tool version.

Only the publish job receives `contents: write`; the build job is read-only. The workflow
refuses to replace an existing release. Use branch/tag protection and required reviews in
GitHub repository settings. No repository, tag, release or workflow run was created by the
local generator, and no review settings are silently provisioned.

Before publishing, make the Rust package version in `Cargo.toml` and manifest version agree,
commit your changes/lockfile, and create a new matching tag. Never overwrite an already
indexed release: publish a new version for changed source, manifest or bytes.

## Submit to a reviewed index

After the GitHub release exists, submit the generated `index-entry.json` in a PR to your
chosen Exaudio index's `index.json`. Append its release to an existing entry when appropriate;
do not duplicate the identity or overwrite an existing version. Maintainers independently
review repository ownership, tag/source correspondence, license, permissions and actual
release bytes. Index inclusion is **not automatic** and this workflow does not use a token
to modify another repository.

SHA256 binds bytes to an index record; it is not a publisher signature or proof that source
and binary correspond. A developer source remains unreviewed until independently accepted
into the index. The marketplace never executes this repository's source/build scripts:
only your own local toolchain or authorized GitHub workflow builds it.
